# {{.Name}}

A lore project for [lorekeep](https://github.com/gmreyer/lorekeep). Each entity
(a character, a place, an event) is one Markdown file under `world/`. The YAML
frontmatter at the top of each file is the graph; the prose below it is yours.

## Building

Run `lorekeep.exe` and choose **Build**, or from this folder:

```
lorekeep.exe build .
```

`build` checks the whole world and, if it is sound, writes `build/index.db` and
`build/snapshot.json`. **Nothing is written when there are errors.** It exits:

- `0`: the world is valid and the build was written.
- `1`: the world has errors; nothing was written. Read the findings.
- `2`: lorekeep could not read the project, or could not get the lorekeep
  version it pins.

Errors make the graph wrong: a reference to an id that does not exist, or a
relation or entity type nobody declared. Warnings are judgement calls and never
stop a build.

## Layout

```
schema/            this world's vocabulary, on top of lorekeep's core pack
  pack.yaml            name, version, core_version
  entity-types.yaml, relations.yaml, eras.yaml, acts.yaml
world/             authored lore, one entity per file, grouped by type
lorekeep-version   the lorekeep version that builds this project
build/             derived output, safe to delete
```

The project pack starts empty on purpose. The core pack already provides the
structural entity types and relations. Add your own only when your writing needs
them.

## lorekeep versions

`lorekeep-version` names the lorekeep release that builds this project. Whichever
`lorekeep.exe` you start, it runs that release, downloading it the first time if
you agree. When a newer release comes out, lorekeep shows its notes and asks
before moving the project. It moves the project only if the new release builds
it without errors. Don't edit `lorekeep-version` by hand; choose **Update lorekeep
for this project** in the menu, or run `lorekeep.exe update .`.

`core_version` in `schema/pack.yaml` is a different fact: the core vocabulary
the lore was written against. `lorekeep build` reports when the two drift.

## Git

This project works without git. To track it with git, choose **Prepare for
git** in the menu or run `lorekeep.exe git .`; add `-ci` for a GitHub workflow
that builds the world on every push. Keep the folder out of OneDrive: Windows
points `Documents` at OneDrive by default, and a synced repository produces file
locks and phantom conflicts.

See [lorekeep's README](https://github.com/gmreyer/lorekeep#readme) for the full
list of commands.
